<section class="partner-section">
  <div class="partner-section__inner partner-section--narrow">
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="partner-faq">

      <details>
        <summary>How do you treat exertional heat stroke in the prehospital setting?</summary>
        <p>The reference treatment, validated by the ACSM and the IOC, is whole-body cold water immersion started from the moment of first contact with the victim, before any transport. This is the Cool First, Transport Second protocol: cooling takes priority over evacuation until the victim's core temperature drops below 38.6°C.</p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>How fast does cold water immersion cool a patient?</summary>
        <p>Whole-body cold water immersion cools at 0.35°C per minute, compared to roughly 0.03°C per minute for wet towels or ice packs. According to the Korey Stringer Institute, survival reaches 100% across more than 400 documented cases when core temperature drops below 40°C within 30 minutes.</p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>How long does it take to deploy an emergency cooling tub?</summary>
        <p>An emergency cooling tub designed for operational use should be deployable by one person, with no tools, in under 2 minutes. This is a decisive requirement in a prehospital response, where the equipment needs to be operational as soon as the crew reaches the patient.</p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What criteria matter when choosing cooling equipment for an EMS or emergency department?</summary>
        <p>Four criteria matter most: single-operator deployment speed, portability inside an ambulance or as departmental stock, a material that meets hospital hygiene requirements such as food-grade stainless steel, and 360-degree patient access for monitoring throughout immersion.</p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Does a cooling tub fit inside a standard ambulance?</summary>
        <p>Yes, provided it is designed for field use: folded down into a compact, lightweight format, it stows in a standard ambulance with no modification, then deploys directly on scene.</p>
      </details>

    </div>

